# ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
# │ LESSON 1 · The clock and the sine                    │
# └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
#
# Every sound in aither starts with a clock. phasor(220)
# counts from 0 up to 1, over and over, 220 times a second.
# That's all an oscillator is underneath: something that
# counts laps. The shape you read the clock through (a sine,
# a sawtooth...) is what gives the sound its colour.
#
# This file plays the clock on its own for three seconds,
# then reads it through a sine: the purest tone there is,
# one single frequency with no colour at all. A tuning
# fork, a soft flute and a whistle are all close to a sine.
#
# ▶ HOW TO PLAY THIS FILE (from ~/aitherLang)
#
#   ./aither | tools/play plughw:rockchipes8388c
#                                     once per session, in its own
#                                     terminal: the engine, piped
#                                     into the uConsole's speaker
#   ./aither send edc/01_sine.aither  play it (or: edc/learn play 1)
#   ./aither stop 01_sine 1           fade it out over 1 second
#
#   Headphones help: the uConsole's speaker can't play much
#   below ~200 Hz, so low notes will sound thin or vanish.
#
#   edc/learn lists every lesson and checks your puzzles.
#
# ▶ WHAT THE CODE SAYS
#
#   play clock:     a named part. You can mute it, solo it
#                   and fade it by name from the command line.
#   phasor(220)     the clock: 0 → 1, 220 times a second.
#                   Sent to the speaker raw, it's a thin buzz:
#                   a ramp that jumps back to 0 every lap.
#   TAU * ...       one lap is one full turn of a circle, and
#                   TAU (6.28...) is a full turn in the units
#                   sin wants. Lesson 2 is all about it.
#   sin(...)        the shape. The height of a dot going
#                   round a circle: a smooth wave, no buzz.
#   * 0.2           the volume. 1.0 is full scale. Keep the
#                   total of everything under 1.
#   the last line   is what you hear: the clock for the
#                   first three seconds, then the tone.
#
#   sin(TAU * phasor(220)) is so common it has a shorthand,
#   osc(sin, 220). That isn't a special feature: it's one
#   line in stdlib.aither,
#
#     def osc(shape, freq): shape(TAU * phasor(freq))
#
#   and you'll write lines like it yourself from lesson 4.
#   The long way is the real thing; the shorthand just saves
#   typing. The lessons use both.
#
# ▶ FREQUENCY IS PITCH
#
#   220 Hz means the clock laps 220 times a second, so the
#   speaker cone moves back and forth 220 times a second.
#   Double it (440) and you hear the same note an octave
#   higher. Halve it (110) and it drops an octave. Musicians
#   call 440 Hz "A", the note orchestras tune to. 220 is
#   the A below it.

play clock:
  phasor(220) * 0.2

play tone:
  sin(TAU * phasor(220)) * 0.2

if t - start_t < 3 then clock else tone

# ▶ TRY THIS (edit, save, send again)
#
#   1. Change both 220s to 440 and send. The pitch jumps up
#      an octave, with no click: aither keeps the clock
#      running and just changes its speed. That's live
#      coding.
#   2. Change 0.2 to 0.05. Quieter. Volume is multiplication.
#   3. Try 330. That's a "perfect fifth" above 220, because
#      330/220 = 3/2. Simple ratios sound related.
#   4. Try 50. On the speaker you'll hear almost nothing;
#      on headphones it's a deep hum you half-feel.
#   5. Make the last line just `clock` and listen to the
#      raw clock for a while. It's buzzy because of the
#      jump from 1 back to 0 every lap: sharp corners mean
#      bright sound. Lesson 3 explains why.
#   6. Look at the sound instead of hearing it:
#        ./aither audit edc/01_sine.aither 2 --from 3
#      (--from 3 skips the clock.) One peak at 220 Hz and
#      nothing else. That's what "pure" means. Now audit
#      without --from 3: the clock has a peak at every
#      multiple of 220.
#
# ▶ PUZZLE → edc/01_sine_puzzle.aither
